#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091902 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "44f09c22-7107-5c80-9c91-a7b58cb639b2" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "a7f1c431-54b4-52b5-9ad6-cb4dd81e0f30" :question/math-academy-id "e-151" :question/problem "A function $f(x)$ has values according to the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $-1.0$ | $-0.7$ | $-0.3$ | $0.7$ | $1.5$ | $2.1$ |\n\nApproximate at the point $x=3$ using a forward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation formula\n\n$$\nf'(x) \\approx \\frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=3$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nf^{′}(3) \\mid \\approx (f(3 + 1) - f(3))/(1); = (f(4) - f(3))/(1); = ((-0.3) - (-0.7))/(1); = \\frac{0.4}{1}; = 0.4\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091903 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "419134c3-3177-5d7d-86f6-899a73338b1c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/id #uuid "8717eda6-57e6-59ec-ab1c-f40887fa52fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-152" :question/problem "The graph of the function $y=g(x)$ is shown below.\nApproximate $g'(x)$ at the point $x=4$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/f7601d421583e4e520cba5b7a0094276.png)" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ng'(x) \\approx \\frac{g(x+h) - g(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the point $x=4$. Applying the above formula with $x=4$ and $h=2$ gives\n\n$$\ng^{′}(4) \\mid \\approx (g(4 + 2) - g(4))/(2); = (g(6) - g(4))/(2); = \\frac{2 - 6}{2}; = \\frac{-4}{2}; =-2\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091904 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e651cb4-ae24-5ac7-9c01-d90de562b155" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "580533af-c13d-5e1c-8282-f0172709c7e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-153" :question/problem "The position of a race car along a straight track, measured in feet from the starting line, is given by the function $s(t)$, where $t$ is the time in seconds. The values of $s(t)$ for different values of $t$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $s(t)$ | $0$ | $3$ | $7$ | $12$ | $19$ |\n\nApproximate the velocity $s'(t)$ at the time $t=0.1$ using a forward difference approximation." :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\ns'(t) \\approx \\frac{s(t+h) - s(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=0.1$. We choose the step size $h$ to be as small as possible, in this case $h=0.1$. This gives\n\n$$\ns^{′}(0.1) \\mid \\approx (s(0.1 + 0.1) - s(0.1))/(0.1); = (s(0.2) - s(0.1))/(0.1); = \\frac{7 - 3}{0.1}; = \\frac{4}{0.1}; = 40\n$$\n\nSo at $t=0.1$ the velocity is approximately $40\\, \\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186091905 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "167f04f0-c08e-51d8-acfa-2c4660083e6a" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "73818e0b-6f95-59a6-b1a7-672bbea8de86" :question/math-academy-id "e-154" :question/problem "An oil tank has leaked $q(t)$ liters of oil after $t$ hours. The graph of $q(t)$ is shown below. Approximate the rate at which the oil is leaking from the tank when $t=1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h=1$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/b42fd1d22af4ee74a06a5344ab52a8c8.png)" :question/worked-solution "We use the forward difference approximation\n\n$$\nq'(t) \\approx \\frac{q(t+h) - q(t)}{h}\n$$\n\nat the time $t=1$ with step size $h=1$. This gives\n\n$$\nq^{′}(1) \\mid \\approx (q(1 + 1) - q(1))/(1); = (q(2) - q(1))/(1); = 3 - 2; = 1\n$$\n\nSo, after $1$ hour, the tank is leaking oil at a rate of approximately $1\\,\\textrm{liter}/ \\textrm{hour}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092664 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e651cb4-ae24-5ac7-9c01-d90de562b155" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Table Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9afe2e83-8fa2-509a-a4f0-c4e386f18fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8liters/mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090450} {:answer/id #uuid "d0021242-94fd-580a-9fa4-a2d263311fb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.2liters/mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090451} {:answer/id #uuid "154c9ed4-50dd-5af2-95dd-1e06ee41f8b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.6liters/mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090452} {:answer/id #uuid "72e2b97f-23b4-51ae-bc4c-08babf085e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.6liters/mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090453} {:answer/id #uuid "76e3a6c9-c700-5972-a662-5ac8be338a17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.2liters/mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9afe2e83-8fa2-509a-a4f0-c4e386f18fb3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.8liters/mile" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090450} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a34b84-9257-5e81-8109-61045e5b92d1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090449}] :question/id #uuid "95a18afa-f8fa-5bdd-82d9-85c9a941305b" :question/math-academy-id "q-47380" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe function $g(s)$ describes the amount of fuel, in liters, remaining in Samuel's car after driving $s$ miles. The values of $g(s)$ for different values of $s$ are given in the table below:\n\n| $s$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $g(s)$ | $15$ | $13.5$ | $12.1$ | $9.4$ | $8.2$ | $6.6$ | $4.8$ |\n\nApproximate the rate of change in the fuel volume when $s = 8$ miles using a forward difference approximation." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092665 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "167f04f0-c08e-51d8-acfa-2c4660083e6a" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c44c9058-e8db-5e01-b9a8-15ad2d3d119f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25watts/minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090456} {:answer/id #uuid "0f206ed5-510a-5588-9d3b-983c4146c968" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50watts/minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090457} {:answer/id #uuid "48d09120-b0dc-54ba-8ad8-07b9246331a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75watts/minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090458} {:answer/id #uuid "54372f8a-2287-5fe5-ab1b-4fd6193dc29e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.5watts/minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090459} {:answer/id #uuid "585fe65a-e2f6-5920-9347-9c0798b2d72e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.5watts/minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090460}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c44c9058-e8db-5e01-b9a8-15ad2d3d119f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25watts/minute" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090456} :answer-field/id #uuid "117516e0-7f4b-5572-b816-475ac997f243" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090455}] :question/id #uuid "d2e860e6-e569-50bb-bb89-eb16bb320f4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-47383" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/992/Source/Images/q-47383.png)\n\nThe power of a mobile phone emitter increases when the phone is switched on. The power output of the emitter after $t$ minutes is given by $W(t)$, where $W$ is measured in watts. The graph of $W(t)$ is shown above.\nApproximate the rate of increase in the power of the emitter when $t = 1$ using a forward difference approximation with step size $h = 2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092667 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "419134c3-3177-5d7d-86f6-899a73338b1c" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Derivative From a Graph Using a Forward Difference Approximation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6a3f2ac-404b-5dd4-8dae-e9fad7b936d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090468} {:answer/id #uuid "ff855b3b-2c71-5815-a915-22a57d790089" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090469} {:answer/id #uuid "19c8d7f4-042e-5d6a-af86-786c2c8cb05a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090470} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf68d93-4152-5389-9116-6de07ca87ff5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090471} {:answer/id #uuid "3d9a4927-8541-5b5c-a5e1-fb42256a9452" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6a3f2ac-404b-5dd4-8dae-e9fad7b936d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090468} :answer-field/id #uuid "59b488c5-ab65-5395-814b-485f6c2580ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090467}] :question/id #uuid "50a7c893-eb05-5fd8-8db1-690a8664464b" :question/